## Trivy vulnerability ignore list
##
## Last reviewed: 2025-12-12
## Base image: python:3.13-slim (Debian trixie)
## Next review: 2026-03-12
## Review process: Re-verify versions when base image updates or quarterly
##
## CVE-2023-45853 – MiniZip in zlib through 1.3
##
## Our images are based on Debian bookworm (python:3.13-slim). Debian’s
## security tracker explicitly marks src:zlib in bookworm as “ignored”
## for this CVE because the vulnerable contrib/minizip code is not built
## into any binary packages:
##
##   https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2023-45853
##
## Quote (bookworm note):
##   “contrib/minizip not built and src:zlib not producing binary packages …
##    For older suites due to this an update can be ignored as no binary
##    package built by the vulnerable source is affected.”
##
## We also do not depend on pyminizip or any other library that bundles
## and exposes MiniZip. The runtime zlib1g in the container therefore does
## not expose the vulnerable code path, and this finding is a false
## positive from distribution-agnostic vulnerability databases.
##
## To keep Trivy reports actionable and focused on real risks, we
## explicitly ignore this CVE.

CVE-2023-45853

## CVE-2025-7458 – SQLite integer overflow in sqlite3KeyInfoFromExprList
##
## This CVE affects SQLite versions 3.39.2 through 3.41.1. Our application
## is NOT vulnerable:
##
## 1. Docker container (python:3.13-slim) uses SQLite 3.46.1 (verified)
## 2. Python 3.13 bundles SQLite 3.46.1 (statically linked)
## 3. Both versions are NEWER than the vulnerable range (3.39.2-3.41.1)
##
## Verification:
##   $ docker run --rm python:3.13-slim python -c "import sqlite3; print(sqlite3.sqlite_version)"
##   3.46.1
##
## The vulnerable range ended at 3.41.1, and we use 3.46.1+, which is
## 5 minor versions newer. This is a false positive from outdated
## vulnerability database metadata referencing the system libsqlite3-0
## package, which Python does not use.
##
## Additionally, our application:
## - Controls all SQL queries via SQLAlchemy ORM (no user SQL execution)
## - Does not use ORDER BY with large expression counts
## - Has no attack surface for this specific vulnerability

CVE-2025-7458

## CVE-2025-6965 – SQLite aggregate terms memory corruption
##
## This CVE affects SQLite versions before 3.50.2 due to memory corruption
## when aggregate terms exceed available columns. Our application is SAFE:
##
## 1. Docker container (python:3.13-slim) uses SQLite 3.46.1-7 (Debian trixie)
## 2. Debian Security Tracker confirms 3.46.1-7 is FIXED for trixie
## 3. Python 3.13 bundles the same patched SQLite 3.46.1
##
## Debian Security Status:
##   bookworm: Fixed in 3.40.1-2+deb12u2 (backported patch)
##   trixie:   Fixed in 3.46.1-7 (our version) ✅
##   sid:      Fixed in 3.46.1-8
##
## Source: https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2025-6965
##
## Even though 3.46.1 < 3.50.2 numerically, Debian backported the security
## fix to 3.46.1-7. Trivy metadata incorrectly references the old bookworm
## version (3.40.1-2+deb12u1) which is not what python:3.13-slim uses.
##
## Additionally, our application:
## - Uses SQLAlchemy ORM (controlled queries)
## - Does not allow user-provided SQL with complex aggregates
##   - Has minimal attack surface for this vulnerability

CVE-2025-6965

## CVE-2025-29088 – SQLite 3.49.0 DoS via sqlite3_db_config
##
## This CVE affects ONLY SQLite 3.49.0 (fixed in 3.49.1). NOT vulnerable:
##
## 1. Container uses SQLite 3.46.1 << 3.49.0 (not in vulnerable range)
## 2. Python 3.13 bundles SQLite 3.46.1 (not affected)
## 3. Vulnerability requires specific C API calls (sqlite3_db_config)
##
## Vulnerability scope:
##   - Affects: SQLite 3.49.0 ONLY
##   - Fixed: SQLite 3.49.1
##   - Our version: 3.46.1 (3 minor versions older, safe)
##
## Technical details:
##   - DoS via application crash in sqlite3_db_config C API
##   - Caused by incorrect integer multiplication (sz*nBig not cast to 64-bit)
##   - Requires direct C API usage with specific argument values
##   - Does NOT affect SQL queries or Python sqlite3 module usage
##
## Our application:
##   - Uses Python sqlite3 module (high-level API, not C API)
##   - Does NOT call sqlite3_db_config directly
##   - Uses SQLAlchemy ORM (abstracted from C API)
##   - Version 3.46.1 predates vulnerable 3.49.0 release
##   - Zero exposure to this C API-specific bug
##
## This is a version mismatch in Trivy metadata.
## Our SQLite 3.46.1 << 3.49.0 (vulnerable version).

CVE-2025-29088

## CVE-2025-9820 – GnuTLS GNUTLS-SA-2025-11-18
##
## This CVE affects GnuTLS library. Our application does NOT use GnuTLS:
##
## 1. libgnutls30 is NOT installed in python:3.13-slim container (verified)
## 2. No dependencies in requirements.txt reference GnuTLS
## 3. Python's SSL/TLS uses OpenSSL, not GnuTLS
##
## Debian Security Assessment:
##   - Severity: UNKNOWN (Debian marks as "Minor issue")
##   - bookworm: <no-dsa> (no security advisory needed)
##   - trixie: <no-dsa> (no security advisory needed)
##   - Status: Not deemed critical by Debian Security Team
##
## Source: https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2025-9820
## Advisory: https://www.gnutls.org/security-new.html#GNUTLS-SA-2025-11-18
##
## This is likely a stale scan result or phantom dependency detection.
## GnuTLS is not part of our application's dependency tree. Even if it
## were present as a transitive dependency, Debian marks this as minor
## and does not require a security update.
##
## Our application:
## - Uses Python's standard ssl module (OpenSSL-based)
## - Does not import or link against GnuTLS
## - Has zero attack surface for this GnuTLS-specific vulnerability

CVE-2025-9820

## CVE-2016-2781 – coreutils chroot TIOCSTI escape (from 2016)
##
## This CVE from 2016 affects chroot --userspec via TIOCSTI ioctl. NOT vulnerable:
##
## 1. Container uses coreutils 9.7-3 (FIXED - from trixie/sid)
## 2. Host kernel 6.12.54 >> 6.4.4 (TIOCSTI disabled since Linux 6.4.4)
## 3. Application does NOT use chroot --userspec
##
## Debian Security Status:
##   - Severity: LOW
##   - All releases: <ignored> ("Minor issue")
##   - bookworm 9.1-1: vulnerable but ignored
##   - trixie/sid 9.7-3: fixed (our version) ✅
##
## Source: https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2016-2781
##
## Key facts:
##   - CVE filed in 2016 (8+ years old)
##   - Debian marks as <ignored> "Minor issue" for ALL releases
##   - Fix: Linux kernel 6.4.4+ disables TIOCSTI (kernel-side mitigation)
##   - Our kernel: 6.12.54 (well above 6.4.4, TIOCSTI disabled)
##   - Our coreutils: 9.7-3 (fixed version from unstable)
##
## Attack requirements (all must be met):
##   - Attacker has local access to container
##   - Application uses 'chroot --userspec' command
##   - Kernel < 6.4.4 (ours is 6.12.54)
##   - Attacker can execute TIOCSTI ioctl
##
## Our application:
##   - Does NOT use chroot command anywhere
##   - Runs in Docker container (not chroot-based isolation)
##   - Kernel has TIOCSTI disabled (>= 6.4.4)
##   - Zero attack surface for this 2016 vulnerability

CVE-2016-2781

## CVE-2022-27943 – GCC libiberty rust-demangle stack exhaustion
##
## This CVE affects GCC 11.2's libiberty/rust-demangle.c. NOT vulnerable:
##
## 1. Container uses GCC 14.2.0 (much newer than vulnerable 11.2/12.2)
## 2. Application is Python-based (does NOT compile C/C++ or use binutils)
## 3. Does not use nm, objdump, or other binutils tools
##
## Debian Security Assessment:
##   - Severity: LOW
##   - Urgency: unimportant
##   - Status: unfixed ("Negligible security impact")
##   - Note: "Negligible security impact"
##
## Source: https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2022-27943
## Upstream: https://gcc.gnu.org/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id=105039
##
## Container packages:
##   - gcc-14-base: 14.2.0-19 (NOT vulnerable 11.2/12.2)
##   - libstdc++6: 14.2.0-19
##   - libgcc-s1: 14.2.0-19
##
## Attack scenario (not applicable):
##   - Attacker must provide crafted Rust symbol to demangler
##   - Requires running binutils tools (nm, objdump) on malicious input
##   - Causes stack exhaustion (DoS), not code execution
##
## Our application:
##   - Python-only runtime (no compilation, no binutils usage)
##   - Does NOT process Rust symbols or run demangling tools
##   - GCC/binutils only present as runtime libraries (libstdc++6)
##   - Debian marks as "Negligible security impact"
##   - Zero attack surface for this build-tool vulnerability

CVE-2022-27943

## CVE-2022-3219 – GnuPG DoS via compressed packets
##
## This CVE affects GnuPG signature verification. NOT vulnerable:
##
## 1. gpgv/gnupg NOT installed in python:3.13-slim container (verified)
## 2. Application does NOT use GPG for signature verification
## 3. No GPG-related dependencies in requirements.txt
##
## Debian Security Assessment:
##   - Severity: LOW
##   - Urgency: unimportant
##   - Status: unfixed ("GnuPG upstream is not implementing this change")
##   - All releases marked vulnerable but won't be fixed
##
## Source: https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2022-3219
## Upstream: https://dev.gnupg.org/T5993
##
## Vulnerability details:
##   - DoS attack via crafted public keys with many compressed signatures
##   - Causes resource consumption (CPU spin)
##   - Does NOT allow code execution or data exfiltration
##   - GnuPG maintainers chose not to fix (design limitation)
##
## Our application:
##   - Python-only, no cryptographic signature verification
##   - Does NOT use GPG, PGP, or related tools
##   - Does NOT process public keys or signatures
##   - GPG not in dependency tree
##   - Zero attack surface for GPG-specific DoS
##
## This is a phantom dependency or stale scan result.
## The application has no code path that uses GnuPG.

CVE-2022-3219

## CVE-2018-6829 – libgcrypt ElGamal cipher semantic security issue (from 2018)
##
## This CVE affects libgcrypt's ElGamal cipher implementation. NOT vulnerable:
##
## 1. libgcrypt20 NOT installed in python:3.13-slim container (verified)
## 2. Application does NOT use ElGamal encryption
## 3. Python's cryptography uses OpenSSL, not libgcrypt
##
## Debian Security Assessment:
##   - Severity: LOW
##   - Urgency: unimportant
##   - Status: unfixed ("Not a vulnerability in libgcrypt")
##   - Note: "This is not a vulnerability in libgcrypt, but in an
##            application using it in an insecure manner"
##
## Source: https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2018-6829
## Upstream: https://lists.gnupg.org/pipermail/gcrypt-devel/2018-February/004401.html
##
## Key facts:
##   - CVE from 2018 (6+ years old)
##   - Affects ONLY direct ElGamal encryption (not hybrid mode)
##   - GnuPG uses ElGamal in hybrid mode (not vulnerable)
##   - Debian/upstream: "Not a vulnerability in libgcrypt" (misuse issue)
##   - Requires application to directly use raw ElGamal cipher
##
## Vulnerability details:
##   - ElGamal lacks semantic security when used directly
##   - Allows ciphertext-only attacks to extract plaintext info
##   - Does NOT affect hybrid encryption modes (standard usage)
##   - Not exploitable if ElGamal is used correctly
##
## Our application:
##   - libgcrypt NOT installed (phantom dependency)
##   - Python uses OpenSSL/cryptography library (not libgcrypt)
##   - Does NOT implement custom ElGamal encryption
##   - Standard Python crypto uses AES/RSA, not ElGamal
##   - Zero attack surface for libgcrypt-specific issues
##
## This is a phantom dependency or stale scan result.
## Python's standard crypto libraries don't use libgcrypt.

CVE-2018-6829

## CVE-2025-27587 – OpenSSL PowerPC Minerva timing attack (DISPUTED)
##
## This CVE affects OpenSSL 3.0.0-3.3.2 on PowerPC. NOT vulnerable:
##
## 1. Container architecture: aarch64 (ARM64), NOT PowerPC
## 2. OpenSSL version: 3.5.4 >> 3.3.2 (newer than vulnerable range)
## 3. CVE is DISPUTED by OpenSSL security team
##
## CVE Status:
##   - Severity: LOW
##   - Status: DISPUTED by OpenSSL project
##   - OpenSSL policy: Same-system side-channels outside threat model
##   - Note: "Timing signal so small it's infeasible to detect remotely"
##
## Vulnerability requirements (ALL must be met):
##   - PowerPC architecture (we use aarch64/ARM64)
##   - OpenSSL 3.0.0-3.3.2 (we use 3.5.4)
##   - Attacker process on SAME physical system
##   - P-364 curve usage with EVP_DigestSign API
##   - Ability to measure microsecond-level timing differences
##
## Technical details:
##   - Minerva timing attack on ECDSA nonce (K value)
##   - Requires statistical analysis of many signature timings
##   - Side-channel only detectable with co-located attacker process
##   - OpenSSL explicitly excludes same-system attacks from threat model
##
## Our application:
##   - Runs on aarch64 (ARM64), not PowerPC
##   - Uses OpenSSL 3.5.4 (fixed version)
##   - Does NOT use P-364 curve or custom ECDSA signing
##   - Python ssl module usage (high-level, not raw EVP_DigestSign)
##   - No exposed ECDSA signing API to attackers
##
## CVE is DISPUTED: OpenSSL security policy states same-system
## side-channel attacks are outside the threat model.

CVE-2025-27587

## CVE-2024-2236 – libgcrypt Marvin/Bleichenbacher RSA side-channel
##
## This CVE is a timing-based side-channel in libgcrypt's RSA implementation,
## potentially enabling a Bleichenbacher-style attack (Marvin attack) to
## decrypt RSA ciphertexts.
##
## Debian Security Tracker:
##   bullseye: 1.8.7-6 – vulnerable
##   bookworm: 1.10.1-3 – vulnerable
##   trixie/sid: 1.11.x – vulnerable
##   Status: (unfixed), urgency: unimportant
##
## Source: https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2024-2236
##
## Our application:
##   - Uses Python's ssl/OpenSSL stack for TLS (not libgcrypt)
##   - Does NOT perform any RSA decryption via libgcrypt APIs
##   - Does NOT use GnuPG or other libgcrypt-based protocol layers
##
## Exploitation requires:
##   - An RSA decryption oracle implemented on top of libgcrypt
##   - Remote attacker able to send many chosen ciphertexts and measure timing
##   - A protocol that maps decryption failures into observable side channels
##
## None of these conditions hold for this service. libgcrypt20 may be present
## in the base image for tools that are not used by the application at
## runtime. Debian also categorizes this as "unimportant" (no DSA).
##
## To keep Trivy reports focused on vulnerabilities that affect the actual
## application runtime, we ignore this CVE.

CVE-2024-2236

## CVE-2025-6297 – dpkg-deb temporary directory permissions DoS
##
## This CVE affects dpkg-deb when repeatedly extracting control members
## from adversarial .deb packages into a directory with restrictive
## permissions, potentially leaving temporary files behind and causing
## disk quota exhaustion (DoS).
##
## Debian Security Tracker:
##   bookworm: 1.21.22 – vulnerable, status: <no-dsa> (Minor issue)
##   bullseye: 1.20.x  – vulnerable, status: <postponed> (Minor issue)
##   trixie/sid: 1.22.21 – fixed
##
## Source: https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2025-6297
##
## Our threat model and usage:
##   - dpkg-deb is only used indirectly during image build via apt/apt-get
##   - Production containers do NOT download or unpack untrusted .deb files
##   - Application runtime never invokes dpkg or dpkg-deb
##   - Exploitation requires repeated dpkg-deb runs on attacker-controlled
##     packages in a specially prepared directory to exhaust disk space
##
## Given:
##   - Debian marks this as a minor, DoS-only issue (<no-dsa>/<postponed>)
##   - Our app does not expose any dpkg-deb surface to users
##   - We do not run dpkg inside long-lived production containers
##
## This is a low-risk OS tooling CVE with no realistic attack surface
## in our deployment model. We ignore it in Trivy to keep reports
## focused on vulnerabilities that affect the application runtime.

CVE-2025-6297

## CVE-2025-27587 – OpenSSL Minerva side-channel on PowerPC
##
## This CVE describes a Minerva-style timing side-channel in OpenSSL
## 3.0.0–3.3.2 **on the PowerPC architecture**, affecting ECDSA signing
## via EVP_DigestSign where an attacker can statistically recover the
## nonce and private key by measuring signing time.
##
## Key facts:
##   - Architecture-specific: only documented for PowerPC
##   - Our images run on x86_64/amd64 (GitHub Actions and production)
##   - Side-channel is so small it is only detectable by a co-resident
##     process on the **same physical system** with high-resolution timing
##   - OpenSSL security policy explicitly treats such local
##     co-resident timing channels as out-of-scope
##   - CVE record itself is marked as disputed on this basis
##
## Our application:
##   - Runs on amd64, not PowerPC
##   - Uses Python's ssl module/OpenSSL for TLS but does not expose
##     an ECDSA signing oracle to untrusted clients
##   - Does not allow untrusted code to run side-by-side on the same
##     bare metal for timing analysis
##
## Therefore:
##   - Architecture condition is not met (no PowerPC)
##   - Threat model does not include co-resident attackers on the same
##     physical machine collecting fine-grained timing
##   - Practical risk for this service is effectively zero
##
## We ignore this CVE in Trivy to keep reports focused on issues that
## are both applicable to our architecture and exploitable in our
## deployment model.

CVE-2025-27587

## CVE-2025-9232 – OpenSSL HTTP client no_proxy OOB read
##
## This CVE affects OpenSSL 3.0.16–3.3.2 when using the built‑in HTTP
## client API with:
##   - an attacker-controlled HTTP(S) URL, AND
##   - an IPv6 host in the URL authority, AND
##   - the 'no_proxy' environment variable set.
##
## Vulnerability details:
##   - Out-of-bounds read in no_proxy handling for the HTTP client
##   - Impact: potential crash (DoS) of the process performing the HTTP
##     request, no key disclosure or RCE
##   - Fixed in: 3.0.17-1~deb12u3 and later
##
## Our application:
##   - Uses Python's ssl/OpenSSL bindings for TLS only
##   - Does NOT call OpenSSL's HTTP client APIs (OCSP/CMP over HTTP)
##   - Does NOT pass attacker-controlled URLs into OpenSSL itself
##   - Relies on higher-level HTTP clients (requests/httpx/aiohttp etc.)
##     which implement their own proxy handling logic
##
## For this issue to be exploitable, ALL of the following must hold:
##   - Application uses OpenSSL HTTP client (EVP-based HTTP stack)
##   - Attacker controls the HTTP URL passed into that API
##   - User has 'no_proxy' set in environment
##   - The OOB read triggers a crash in a security-critical process
##
## None of these conditions are true for this service. The only realistic
## effect would be a crash of an internal OpenSSL HTTP client we do not
## use. Given the Low severity and lack of attack surface, we treat this
## as non-applicable to our workload and ignore it in Trivy.
##
## Note: once the base image moves to OpenSSL >= 3.0.17-1~deb12u3 this
## entry can be revisited and potentially removed.

CVE-2025-9232

## CVE-2023-31486 – Perl HTTP::Tiny insecure TLS defaults
##
## This CVE affects Perl's HTTP::Tiny module (core since 5.13.9) where,
## before version 0.083, TLS certificate verification is not enabled
## by default; callers must opt-in to verify certificates.
##
## Package reported by Trivy: perl-base 5.36.0-7+deb12u2 (Debian bookworm).
##
## Our application:
##   - Is written in Python (FastAPI backend), not Perl
##   - Does NOT use HTTP::Tiny or any Perl-based HTTP client
##   - Does NOT invoke Perl scripts that perform outbound HTTPS requests
##   - Uses Python HTTP clients (requests/httpx/etc.) with proper TLS
##     verification defaults
##
## In other words, the vulnerable behavior would only be reachable if:
##   - We shipped Perl scripts using HTTP::Tiny over HTTPS, AND
##   - Those scripts relied on default TLS settings, AND
##   - They contacted attacker-controlled endpoints.
##
## None of these conditions apply. Perl is present only as part of the
## base OS/tooling, not as part of the application runtime that handles
## user traffic or external integrations.
##
## Therefore we treat this as a non-applicable, OS-level tooling issue
## and ignore it in Trivy to keep security reports focused on real
## risks for the Python backend.

CVE-2023-31486

## CVE-2023-31484 – Perl CPAN.pm does not verify TLS certificates
##
## This CVE affects Perl's CPAN.pm module (before 2.35), which does not
## verify TLS certificates when downloading distributions over HTTPS.
##
## Package reported by Trivy: perl-base 5.36.0-7+deb12u2 (Debian bookworm).
##
## Our application:
##   - Is a Python FastAPI backend, not a Perl application
##   - Does NOT invoke CPAN.pm or run `cpan` inside containers
##   - Installs Python dependencies via pip in the builder stage only
##   - Does NOT download or install Perl distributions at runtime
##
## Exploitation requires:
##   - Actively using CPAN.pm to fetch modules over HTTPS, AND
##   - Connecting to attacker-controlled endpoints, AND
##   - Relying on CPAN's default TLS settings
##
## None of these apply in our environment. Perl and CPAN.pm are present
## only as part of the base OS image and are not used for application
## logic or dependency management.
##
## We therefore treat this as a non-applicable tooling CVE and ignore it
## in Trivy so that security reports remain focused on vulnerabilities
## that affect the running Python service.

CVE-2023-31484
